description:
src/event.rs:341-346 and the four other /set handlers all start with:

    let account_id = extract_account_id(&args)?;
    let Value::Object(mut args) = args else {
        return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments("arguments must be a JSON object"));
    };

extract_account_id (jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:25) already calls args.get("accountId")... which only succeeds for JSON Object inputs (.get on Value::String returns None). So the second pattern-match is a redundant defensive check — it can never fail after the first call has succeeded. Worse, if the first call failed, the user gets 'accountId is required' (a misleading error for a non-object payload like '"foo"' or '[]').

Two cleanups:
1. Make extract_account_id reject non-object inputs with a clearer message: 'arguments must be an object containing accountId'. Then drop the second pattern-match in every handler.
2. Or invert: have extract_account_id return (Id, &serde_json::Map) so the handler doesn't need to re-destructure the Value.

The canonical extension-server jmap-mail-server has the same pattern, so coordinating the change across both crates (and propagating to every extension-server sibling) is a cookie-cutter consistency requirement per workspace AGENTS.md.

Trivial; P3 because it doesn't affect wire output, only error message clarity for one edge case (caller passes a non-object as args). The dispatcher in jmap-server may guard against this upstream — worth checking before fixing.
